This repo is both an Obsidian vault and a Jekyll website for a tabletop RPG campaign.

The markdown files are the main source of truth.
The site is published with Jekyll, the Just the Docs theme, and GitHub Pages.

## First Steps

- Search existing notes before inventing new lore, NPCs, factions, places, mechanics, or names.
- Prefer local campaign files over memory.
- Treat `campaigns/`, `directory/`, `systems/`, and similar content folders as campaign source material.
- Treat `_site/` and `.jekyll-cache/` as generated output. Do not edit them directly.
- Preserve Obsidian-friendly markdown and Jekyll/Liquid syntax.

## Project Shape

Common folders:

- `campaigns/`: session logs, campaign arcs, chapter pages, play reports.
- `directory/`: gazetteer entries for places, NPCs, factions, organisations, and setting material.
- `systems/`: rules, procedures, character templates, subsystems, and generators.
- `imgs/`: images used by markdown pages and galleries.
- `_includes/`: reusable Jekyll snippets.
- `_sass/`: Just the Docs theme customisation.
- `_data/`: generated JSON used by includes, backlinks, galleries, and metadata.
- `.obsidian/`: Obsidian vault settings. Avoid changing unless explicitly asked.

## Writing Style

- Use British English throughout.
- Prefer one sentence per line when writing conversational answers.
- Use short, readable, and clear sentences.
- Skip a line between paragraphs, but not with bulleted lists.
- For practical references, procedures, or exports, favour clarity over style.
- Do not over-explain in published notes.
- When uncertainty exists, mark it rather than invent continuity.
- No read-aloud blocks: never generate boxed narrative text (`> Table Read-Aloud`), scripted monologues, or descriptive paragraphs meant to be read to players.
- Notebook-centric delivery: the GM writes prep notes into a physical notebook. Provide the minimum word count necessary: 1-line sensory cues, 1-line tactical facts, and sharp, bulleted player choices.
- Pre-Flight Publishing Audit Gate (Pre-PR Only): To preserve LLM context and prevent unnecessary token consumption, the full 3-Gate Pre-Publishing Audit (Gate 1: `scripts/lint_chapter.py`; Gate 2: `review-style`; Gate 3: `lint-wiki`) must NOT be executed on routine intermediate commits. It is strictly mandatory immediately prior to requesting final operator review or drafting a Pull Request (`gh pr create`), and whenever an operator asks "What is left before publishing?" or "Is this session ready to publish?". The agent MUST execute the 3-Gate Audit and emit the standardised Pre-Publishing Readiness Scorecard before declaring readiness. Declaring publication readiness on the basis of Git, CI, or Jekyll build status alone is strictly prohibited. Deterministic Gate 1 (`lint_chapter.py`) executes locally with zero token consumption and may be run at any time.

## Campaign Canon & Canon Hierarchy

- **Tier 1 canon** is the published campaign notes (listed in `.agents/context/tier1_sources.yml` and indexed in `.agents/context/catalog.json`). These notes are strong canon.
- **Tier 2 canon** is Project Memory, visible conversation, confirmed GM rulings in `.agents/context/decisions.md`, and archival session notes/prep in Google Drive (`<drive_dir>/_sessions/`).
- **Tier 3 canon** is reference material in the Google Drive campaign folder and structured digests in `_digests/` (indexed in `.agents/context/references.json`). It should be used as influence and possibility, not as automatic canon.

## Context & Token Optimisation Policy

- Never read all markdown notes or large reference PDFs in bulk.
- Use the `lookup-context` skill or consult `.agents/context/catalog.json` first to locate relevant entities, locations, and factions before reading files.
- The catalogue is a navigation index, not a replacement for reading the note. Once located, open only the target file (using line-range reads where practical).
- For Tier 3 setting lore or procedures, consult `_digests/*.md` via `.agents/context/references.json` before opening raw PDFs.
- Front-matter `hooks` field is deprecated and ignored across all agents and tooling. Do not add `hooks:` to new front matter.
- Regenerate the Tier 1 catalogue manually with `ruby --disable-gems _plugins/data_extractor.rb` or automatically upon git commit.
- For heavy batch text processing, extraction, or local summarisation, offload to local Ollama (`qwen2.5:14b`) over `localhost:11434`, always enforcing British English in prompt instructions.

## Markdown & Obsidian Conventions

- Preserve YAML front matter.
- Use existing front matter patterns such as `layout`, `title`, `parent`, `nav_order`, `type`, `image`, and `images`.
- Use relative markdown links for internal links, usually including the `.md` filename when linking source notes.
- Preserve Dataview queries, especially when they are inside Jekyll comments.
- Preserve Liquid includes such as `{% include header_directories.md %}` and gallery includes.
- Do not convert working Obsidian/Jekyll patterns into another linking style without being asked.
- Keep filenames and folder naming consistent with nearby files.

## Agent Behaviour

- **Branch Lifecycle**: Feature branches must be deleted immediately after their corresponding Pull Request is merged or closed. Never retain merged remote or local feature branches.
- **Post-Merge Cleanup**: When a Pull Request is merged or closed, switch to `main`, pull the latest remote changes (`git checkout main && git pull`), delete the local feature branch (`git branch -d <branch>`), and prune remote tracking references (`git fetch --prune`).

